   "": {
    "actClause": "AN ACT to amend the family court act, in relation to assessment services for youth alleged to be suffering from substance use disorder",
    "basePrintNo": "S7051",
    "basePrintNoStr": "S7051-2013",
    "coSponsors": {
     "items": [],
     "size": 0
    },
    "fullText": "",
    "fullTextFormats": [],
    "fullTextHtml": "",
    "fullTextTemplate": "",
    "lawCode": "Amd §§712 & 735, Fam Ct Act",
    "lawSection": "Family Court Act",
    "memo": "BILL NUMBER:S7051\n\nTITLE OF BILL:  An act to amend the family court act, in relation to\nassessment services for youth alleged to be suffering from substance\nuse disorder\n\nPurpose of Bill:\n\nThis legislation would provide that a youth alleged to be suffering\nfrom substance use disorder may receive an assessment for such\ndisorder as part of Person in Need of Supervision (PINS) diversion\nservices.\n\nSummary of Provisions:\n\nSection 1 amends the definition of \"diversion services\" in section 712\nof the Family Court Act (FCA) § 712 to specify that such services, in\ncases where when the petitioner alleges the child has a substance use\ndisorder or is in need of immediate detoxification or substance use\ndisorder services, may include assessment for substance use disorder.\nThe section also adds new subsections to define \"substance use\ndisorder\" and related terms.\n\nSection 2 amends FCA § 735 to allow the designated lead agency for the\npurpose of providing PINS diversion services (either the local social\nservices district or the local probation department) to determine\nwhether an assessment for substance use disorder by an Office of\nAlcoholism and Substance Abuse Services (OASAS) certified provider of\nservices is necessary in cases where the youth is alleged to be\nsuffering from a substance use disorder which could make the youth a\ndanger to himself or herself or others. The section requires OASAS to\nmake available a list of certified treatment providers to designated\nlead agencies. It also provides that the designated lead agency shall\nnot be required to pay for an assessment for substance use disorder or\nrelated services, except in cases where Medicaid may be used to pay\nfor such assessment or services.\n\nSection 3 provides for an effective date 180 days after the bill\nbecomes law.\n\nExisting Law:\n\nYouth and their families can access a variety of services through the\nPINS diversion process. However, FCA § 712 does not expressly mention\nthat assessment for substance use disorder services can be provided\nthrough the PINS diversion process.\n\nLegislative History:\n\nThis is a new bill.\n\nStatement in Support:\n\nSubstance use disorder among youth, which may include the use of\nalcohol, heroin, prescription drugs, and increasingly, synthetic\ndrugs, is a major concern. This bill clarifies that the existing PINS\ndiversion process is an option available for families who have a child\n\n\nwho may be suffering from a substance use disorder, in order to have a\nneutral third party intercede at what could be a critical point in the\nprogression of a life-threatening disease.\n\nSome parents or guardians of children who abuse drugs or alcohol\ndesire the intervention of an external authority and/or an assessment\nas to whether their child has a substance use disorder. This proposal\nwould explicitly include language in Article 7 of the FCA to provide\nthat substance use disorder services may be utilized as part of the\nPINS diversion process. This bill would facilitate use of the existing\nPINS process as a means through which youth can be assessed for a\nsubstance use disorder. The assessment also may impress upon youth\nthat serious harm could result from continuing use of drugs or\nalcohol.\n\nBudget Implications:\n\nNone.\n\nEffective Date:\n\nThis bill takes effect in 180 days.\n\n",
